/* V52 — the music was not quiet, it was mixed for a speaker nobody has.

   The note was that the background music is very weak. Measuring the music bus rather than guessing
   at it gave a spectrum that explains the complaint completely:

       <200Hz    -56.3 dB
       200-800   -78.9 dB
       800-2.4k -101.6 dB
       2.4-6k   -118.6 dB
       >6k      -134.7 dB

   That is not a quiet mix, it is a mix with essentially nothing in it above two hundred hertz. And a
   phone speaker cannot reproduce below roughly three or four hundred hertz at any volume — the
   driver is a few millimetres across. So almost all of the music's energy was going into the one
   band the target device physically cannot play. Turning the volume up would not have helped; it
   would have made a slightly louder nothing.

   Reading the arrangement confirms it from the other direction. `v30Sub` is called at .34 and
   `v30Kick` at .5, while every melodic and harmonic voice — bass line, pad, hook, sparkle — sits
   between .014 and .075. The sub is fourteen to twenty-seven decibels above the melody. On
   headphones or a laptop that is a perfectly reasonable club-style balance, which is presumably why
   it survived this long; on the device this game is actually played on it means the tune is
   inaudible and only the thump gets through.

   Three things were also darkening what little was left, and they compound:

   1. Every note passes its own resonant lowpass in `v30Voice`, opening at `cutoff` and closing to
      `cutoff*.5` over the note.
   2. The whole bus passes `musicFilter`, which V30 sets to 1700Hz outside combat and
      `2400 + intensity*5200` inside it — so at wave one, with no combo and no boss, the entire
      arrangement is behind a 2.4kHz lowpass.
   3. The bus gain was `curve*0.5`, and a second writer left over from V12 set it to `curve*0.17`,
      which is nine decibels lower again and would win briefly on every settings change.

   So this is a rebalance rather than a new arrangement. Not a note changes, the harmony and the
   tension behaviour V36 tuned are untouched, and the sub stays — it is still what gives the track
   weight on hardware that can produce it. What changes is which part of the spectrum carries the
   tune. */

const V52={
 /* A tilt rather than a flat boost. Nothing below the bass register gains anything, and the lift
    grows with pitch up to three octaves above it, which is the band a phone speaker actually
    reproduces. This is the same shape as a tilt EQ and it is the whole fix in one number. */
 pivot:110,
 octaves:3,
 /* First pass ran this at 2.4 with a .66 sub trim, which moved the bus level by six decibels but
    barely moved the *balance* — the gap to close was twenty-seven decibels and change, so eleven
    was never going to do it. */
 lift:3.2,
 /* The sub and kick are not removed. They come down enough to stop owning the mix, and no further:
    on headphones this track is meant to have a floor. */
 subTrim:.62,
 kickTrim:.7,
 /* The bass pedal was worse than mistuned for the device — it was inaudible on any device. Dumping
    the arrangement's own output shows the pedal running at 13.75, 16.35, 18.35, 21.83 and 24.50Hz,
    because it is written as `bass/4` and the world roots are already low. Human hearing gives out
    around 20Hz. A 13.75Hz sine is not a quiet note, it is not a note; its only audible contribution
    is whatever intermodulation it causes on the way out.

    Anything under this gets lifted, and the lift has to be a *fixed* number of octaves. The first
    attempt doubled in a loop until the note cleared the floor, which mapped the five pedal notes to
    55, 65.4, 36.7, 43.66 and 49Hz — two octaves for some, one for others, which scrambles the
    intervals of the bass line and is a worse bug than the one it fixed. Every sub call in the
    arrangement is `bass/4` or `root/4`, so all of them sit between about 10 and 28Hz; one constant
    two-octave transpose clears the floor for all of them and preserves every pitch relationship in
    the harmony, including V36's held pedal at high tension. 13.75Hz becomes 55Hz: a real bass note,
    audible on headphones, felt on a decent speaker, with harmonics a phone can reproduce. */
 pedalFloor:35,
 pedalTranspose:4,
 /* Octave doubling on the melodic voices — the oldest trick there is for making a lead audible on a
    small speaker, and the reason it works is that the doubled note lands where the driver is
    efficient while the original keeps the register the composition wants.

    Which voices are melodic is inferred from note length, which needs saying out loud because it is
    a heuristic and not a fact: the hook and sparkle run between .8 and 3.2 sixteenths, the pads run
    15 and 30. At this tempo a sixteenth is about 121ms, so anything under three quarters of a second
    is a melody note. Doubling a thirty-sixteenth pad would thicken it into mud, which is exactly
    what this threshold exists to avoid. */
 doubleUnder:.75,
 doubleGain:.42,
 /* Per-note filters open further so the lift has something above the fundamental to actually lift.
    A note boosted behind a closed filter just gets louder and duller. */
 cutoffOpen:2.1
};

/* The lift for a given fundamental: 1x at the pivot, V52.lift x three octaves up, smooth between.
   Above that it flattens off rather than continuing to climb, because a sparkle voice at 4kHz does
   not need six times the level to be heard — it needs the filter out of its way, which it now has. */
function v52Lift(freq){
 const octavesUp=Math.log2(Math.max(1,freq)/V52.pivot);
 const k=Math.max(0,Math.min(1,octavesUp/V52.octaves));
 return 1+(V52.lift-1)*k;
}

/* Assignments, not declarations — V48's lighting was written as a declaration and silently never
   ran, because an assignment elsewhere in the bundle wins regardless of file order. Every override
   here follows the convention the rest of the codebase uses. */
const v52Voice=v30Voice;
v30Voice=function(freq,when,dur,gain,cutoff,pan,detune,type){
 const out=v52Voice(freq,when,dur,gain*v52Lift(freq),Math.min(14000,cutoff*V52.cutoffOpen),pan,detune,type);
 /* The octave up. Panned to the opposite side of its parent so the pair widens rather than simply
    getting louder, and given the same waveform so it reads as the same instrument an octave higher
    instead of as a second instrument playing along. */
 if(dur<V52.doubleUnder&&freq>=150){
  v52Voice(freq*2,when+.004,dur*.92,gain*v52Lift(freq)*V52.doubleGain,
   Math.min(14000,cutoff*V52.cutoffOpen*1.4),-pan*.7,detune,type);
 }
 return out;
};

const v52Sub=v30Sub;
v30Sub=function(freq,when,dur,gain){
 /* One fixed transpose for the whole pedal, applied only to notes that need it. A note already in
    audible range is left where it is rather than pushed out of the bass register. */
 const f=freq<V52.pedalFloor?freq*V52.pedalTranspose:freq;
 return v52Sub(f,when,dur,gain*V52.subTrim);
};

const v52Kick=v30Kick;
v30Kick=function(when,gain){
 return v52Kick(when,gain*V52.kickTrim);
};
